Directive 03a (2026-09-15) - the legacy pi_5 floor, closed. 216 tests pass.

The contract now owns three legacy floors: pi_zero_2w and pi_4 to 2 GB/32 GB,
pi_5 to 4 GB/64 GB. Read-time compatibility only; no tier is buildable again.

The principle worth carrying forward, because it generalises beyond this field:
a compatibility mapping may resolve level or UPWARD, never downward. Resolving
downward silently widens what a box will accept, and the failure appears only
at the moment someone opens the content. Directive 03 was right to refuse to
invent 4 GB and to park the decision here instead.

A second bug found while closing this: the absent-field warning in inspect was
suppressed for ANY pack carrying min_hw_tier, whether or not the value actually
mapped. So a pack with an unmappable tier printed nothing at all about its
hardware requirement. Now it reports the requirement as unknown, and the
suppression is keyed on the mapping having succeeded.

